alloca() is the compiler builtin on NetBSD too.

NetBSD's <stdlib.h> declares `void *alloca(size_t)` for GCC and clang and
defines the __builtin_alloca macro only for PCC, relying on the compiler's
implicit builtin.  Clang has that builtin only in GNU modes, and Mesa builds
with -std=c11 / -std=c++17, so NIR_VLA and every other alloca() here called
libc's alloca(), which moves the stack pointer under code that addresses its
spills relative to it.  nir_from_ssa.c's merge_sets_interfere() (inlined into
aggressive_coalesce_parallel_copy) then reloaded garbage merge-node pointers
and llvmpipe segfaulted compiling VTK's multisample-resolve shader on NetBSD
10.1 (netbsd-build-2, 2026-10-31).  glibc and FreeBSD already define the
macro, so this changes nothing there.  dd_draw.c (the ddebug wrapper) called
alloca() without including c99_alloca.h; it includes it now.

--- a/include/c99_alloca.h
+++ b/include/c99_alloca.h
@@ -43,6 +43,14 @@
 
 #  include <stdlib.h>
 
+/* NetBSD's <stdlib.h> only declares alloca() for GCC and clang, counting on the
+ * compiler's implicit builtin, which strict ISO modes (-std=c11, -std=c++17)
+ * turn off.  The call then goes to libc's alloca(), which moves the caller's
+ * stack pointer behind the compiler's back and corrupts its stack slots. */
+#  if defined(__GNUC__) && !defined(alloca)
+#    define alloca(size) __builtin_alloca(size)
+#  endif
+
 #endif /* !defined(_MSC_VER) */
 
 
--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/driver_ddebug/dd_draw.c
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/driver_ddebug/dd_draw.c
@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@
 #include "util/os_time.h"
 #include <inttypes.h>
 #include "util/detect.h"
+#include "c99_alloca.h"
 
 void
 dd_get_debug_filename_and_mkdir(char *buf, size_t buflen, bool verbose)
